:"Ultimately from Middle French prothoplaste, from Late Latin prōtoplastus (“the first man”), from Koine Greek πρωτόπλαστος (prōtóplastos, “first-formed”), from Ancient Greek πρωτο- (prōto-) + πλαστός (plastós, “formed, moulded”).","livello2_varieta":"la-lat"},{"parola":"sargento","pos":"noun","n_etimologia":1,"categoria":"prestito","incerta":"sì","motivo_incerta":"perhaps; or from","significato":"sergeant","termine_origine":"sergent","catena":[{"codice":"frm","nome":"Middle French","termine":"sergent","categoria":"prestito"},{"codice":"la","nome":"Latin","termine":"serviens","categoria":"ereditato"},{"codice":"itc-pro","nome":"Proto-Italic","termine":"*serwiō","categoria":"ereditato"},{"codice":"ine-pro","nome":"Proto-Indo-European","termine":"","categoria":"ereditato"}],"lontana":"ine-pro","ambiti":["militare"],"via":"","testo":"Inherited from Old Spanish sargente, from Middle French sergent, from Old French sergeant, sergent, serjant, sergient, sergant (“sergeant, servant”), from Medieval Latin servientem (“servant, vassal, soldier, apparitor”), from Latin serviēns (“serving”), present participle of serviō (“to serve or to","livello2_varieta":"la"}]}